cartridge "ACTIONAUTS/ROB FULOP/1986"

' ACTIONAUTS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Actionauts" by Rob Fulop (program design)
' and Lon Richter (graphics and animation), Advanced Program Technology
' 1985, published on the Ahoy! April 1986 disk (Commodore 64; the
' program on the disk is machine code behind a SYS line; orig/ has it and
' jsc64 screenshots). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' What the original is: a robot-programming toy on a slanted 10 x 8 grid
' of lavender squares. Five figures (ROBOT, MOBOT, DROID, ACROBOT,
' RABBIT) each follow their own program of eight commands - ADVANCE 1
' STEP, ADVANCE 2 STEPS, ADVANCE 4 STEPS, TURN LEFT, TURN RIGHT, GOTO
' LINE, PAUSE, CALL FIGURE - entered with the joystick (INSERT, DELETE,
' CLEAR). TEST runs one figure, MANUAL moves it by stick, EDITOR writes
' its program, TOYBOX sets the figures (which square, which way they
' face, or none), GRID changes the playfield with a stamper, ACTION
' starts all figures. Left/right selects the figure, up/down the function.
'
' Not a line-by-line port (the program is machine code and the disk
' carries no rules text): the commands, figures, modes and grid are the
' original's, rebuilt for the ColecoVision: figures glide smoothly as two-
' colour sprites, nearer figures drawn in front, a grid of slanted tiles,
' sound for every action, a title tune played once, a full-screen program
' editor.
'
' Rules as made here (the original's behaviour could not be read from
' its text, so these are choices, kept simple): each command takes its
' time (a step 256/speed frames, a turn 8, PAUSE 16, GOTO and CALL 4); a
' figure walking onto a hole or off the edge falls and is out of the run;
' a figure blocked by another (standing there or walking there) bumps and
' drops the rest of that ADVANCE; CALL FIGURE starts the named figure from
' its first line (if it is standing still); a program that runs past its
' last line stops the figure; the button stops a run, and every run
' starts again from the set-up.
'
' Additions: TESTS, eight puzzles - collect every star on the grid with
' programs no longer than the test's limit (in a test only the figures
' named start with ACTION; the others wait to be CALLed); runs are
' counted. TOYBOX is the original's free mode; its DESIGN joins the
' original's TOYBOX and GRID (one stamper places holes, stars and
' figures and turns them). Speeds differ by figure (MOBOT slow, DROID
' fast, RABBIT fastest, hopping). The toybox's programs and grid are
' reset to the demo when it is entered from the title.
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller). Workspace: left/right
' choose the figure, up/down the function, a button does it. Editor:
' up/down choose the line, left/right change the command, hold the left
' button with left/right to change the number (GOTO line, CALL figure),
' the right button opens INSERT/DELETE/CLEAR/DONE. Design: the stick
' moves the stamper, the left button stamps (or turns the figure there),
' the right button puts or takes away the selected figure, down off the
' grid is DONE. During a run a button stops it; in MANUAL up steps,
' left/right turn.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' screens drawn with the NMI off and the display off; the title tune
' played once, quieter than the effects; `set sprite count` after every
' `clear sprites`; nothing flashes.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs actnaut-for-end-return removed 2026-11-01.

